1
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:07,342
THEME SONG: In the not
too distant future--

2
00:00:07,442 --> 00:00:13,515
next Sunday AD-- there
was a guy named Joel,

3
00:00:13,615 --> 00:00:16,718
not too different
from you or me.

4
00:00:16,818 --> 00:00:21,255
He worked at Gizmonic
Institute, just another face

5
00:00:21,355 --> 00:00:25,993
in a red jumpsuit, he did a
good job cleaning up the place,

6
00:00:26,093 --> 00:00:29,397
but his bosses didn't like him,
so they shot him into space.

7
00:00:29,497 --> 00:00:32,333


8
00:00:32,433 --> 00:00:38,440
We'll send him cheesy movies,
the worst we can find.

9
00:00:38,540 --> 00:00:41,743
He'll have to sit
and watch them all,

10
00:00:41,843 --> 00:00:44,512
and we'll monitor his mind.

11
00:00:44,612 --> 00:00:47,648
Now keep in mind,
Joel can't control

12
00:00:47,748 --> 00:00:51,953
where the movies begin
or end, because he

13
00:00:52,053 --> 00:00:56,523
used those special parts
to make his robot friends.

14
00:00:56,623 --> 00:00:59,393


15
00:00:59,493 --> 00:01:02,230
Robot Roll Call-- Cambot!

16
00:01:02,330 --> 00:01:03,197
Gypsy!

17
00:01:03,297 --> 00:01:05,100
Tom Servo!

18
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:06,467
Croooow!

19
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:09,703
If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes

20
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:14,108
and other science facts,
then repeat to yourself,

21
00:01:14,208 --> 00:01:17,545
it's just a show, I
should really just

22
00:01:17,645 --> 00:01:35,263
relax for Mystery
Science Theater 3000!

23
00:01:35,363 --> 00:01:36,865
-Crow, look, here he comes!

24
00:01:36,965 --> 00:01:38,567
It's Joel!
-Joel!

25
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:39,500
It's Joel, Joel, Joel!

26
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,135
[cheering]

27
00:01:41,235 --> 00:01:41,803
-Hi, you guys.

28
00:01:41,903 --> 00:01:42,903
How you doing?

29
00:01:42,970 --> 00:01:44,438
-Oh, you know,
it's just like you

30
00:01:44,538 --> 00:01:46,307
to think of us
instead of yourself.

31
00:01:46,407 --> 00:01:48,477
-That's what separates
you from us measly bots.

32
00:01:48,577 --> 00:01:49,977
-Thanks.
Hi, everybody.

33
00:01:50,077 --> 00:01:51,312
Welcome to the
Satellite of Love.

34
00:01:51,412 --> 00:01:52,647
I'm Joel Robinson
and these are--

35
00:01:52,747 --> 00:01:53,347
-Oh, ya see?

36
00:01:53,447 --> 00:01:54,348
He introduced himself.

37
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:56,183
What a perfectly
thoughtful thing to do.

38
00:01:56,283 --> 00:01:57,252
-Oh yes, thoughtful.

39
00:01:57,352 --> 00:01:58,720
-These are my robots
Tom Servo and Crow.

40
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:00,988
And I've installed in them
a protocol module that

41
00:02:01,088 --> 00:02:03,057
makes them believe
everything I think, say or do

42
00:02:03,157 --> 00:02:03,958
is utterly brilliant.

43
00:02:04,058 --> 00:02:05,125
-Oh, and it is, Joel.

44
00:02:05,227 --> 00:02:05,827
God, yes.

45
00:02:05,927 --> 00:02:07,128
Oh ho ho, joy!

46
00:02:07,228 --> 00:02:08,830
-Roger Penrose on his best
day isn't half as brilliant

47
00:02:08,930 --> 00:02:10,832
or as charming as
you, I might add.

48
00:02:10,932 --> 00:02:12,967
-Commercial sign in 15 seconds.

49
00:02:13,067 --> 00:02:15,203
Of course, every
blissful second with you

50
00:02:15,303 --> 00:02:17,138
is like an apple of gold, Joel.

51
00:02:17,238 --> 00:02:17,905
-Ah!

52
00:02:18,005 --> 00:02:19,273
-Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes.

53
00:02:19,373 --> 00:02:20,842
-You know, I'm starting to have
second thoughts about this.

54
00:02:20,942 --> 00:02:22,077
I don't know what I think.

55
00:02:22,177 --> 00:02:23,143
-Oh, Joel!

56
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:25,112
I've wiped away every
bit of hesitation

57
00:02:25,213 --> 00:02:26,347
I had about this project.

58
00:02:26,447 --> 00:02:27,948
-Not that we'd take issue
with anything you'd think.

59
00:02:28,048 --> 00:02:29,517
-Oh, no no no no.
-No no no.

60
00:02:29,617 --> 00:02:31,318
-It does make it
easier to work with.

61
00:02:31,418 --> 00:02:33,187
-Oh yes yes, easy as pie!

62
00:02:33,287 --> 00:02:34,088
Brilliant, brilliant.

63
00:02:34,188 --> 00:02:35,155
-More Evian, Mr. Miller?

64
00:02:35,257 --> 00:02:37,125
-It leaves me feeling
kind of hollow.

65
00:02:37,225 --> 00:02:38,258
-Wow.

66
00:02:38,358 --> 00:02:39,960
How insightful of you to
find that in yourself.

67
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:42,363
-You always cut right to
the heart of things, Joel.

68
00:02:42,463 --> 00:02:43,463
-Ah, yeah.

69
00:02:43,565 --> 00:02:44,532
-Yeah, I'm going to
remove those modules.

70
00:02:44,632 --> 00:02:45,733
-Oh, no no.

71
00:02:45,833 --> 00:02:47,335
No, just, just, just, just
shut me down completely.

72
00:02:47,435 --> 00:02:48,302
-Oh!

73
00:02:48,402 --> 00:02:49,670
Uh, jettison me
into space, please!

74
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,738
-Oh, yes, disconnect
with extreme prejudice.

75
00:02:51,838 --> 00:02:52,840
-You see this butt?

76
00:02:52,940 --> 00:02:53,675
Kick this butt.

77
00:02:53,775 --> 00:02:54,508
-We'll be right back.

78
00:02:54,608 --> 00:03:00,882


79
00:03:00,982 --> 00:03:02,683
All right, there's
the protocol module.

80
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:03,852
-Don't touch me.

81
00:03:03,952 --> 00:03:05,520
Don't ever touch me.
-This is painful.

82
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:06,287
That-- ah!

83
00:03:06,387 --> 00:03:07,622
Ungh.
[sniffing]

84
00:03:07,722 --> 00:03:08,890
-Jeez, Joel.

85
00:03:08,990 --> 00:03:11,292
Some small animal crawl
inside your jumpsuit and die?

86
00:03:11,392 --> 00:03:13,562
-There's the bots
I know and love.

87
00:03:13,662 --> 00:03:14,962
Uh-oh, fasten your seatbelts.

88
00:03:15,062 --> 00:03:16,863
Crate & Barrel are calling.

89
00:03:16,965 --> 00:03:19,233
[humming "la marseillaise"]

90
00:03:19,333 --> 00:03:20,235
-Huh?

91
00:03:20,335 --> 00:03:21,468
Oh, Joel.

92
00:03:21,568 --> 00:03:24,005
A hearty hello to you and yours.

93
00:03:24,105 --> 00:03:26,975
Let me just say this about
today's invention exchange--

94
00:03:27,075 --> 00:03:28,810
let them eat chocolate.

95
00:03:28,910 --> 00:03:32,380
-That's right, J--
That's right, Joel.

96
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:36,050
Our invention this week is based
on one's natural inclination

97
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:38,687
to bite the heads off
of chocolate bunnies.

98
00:03:38,787 --> 00:03:40,053
-That's right, Frank.

99
00:03:40,153 --> 00:03:43,257
That's why we've invented the
chocolate bunny guillotine.

100
00:03:43,357 --> 00:03:47,462
Eliminate the guesswork in
biting the heads off bunnies.

101
00:03:47,562 --> 00:03:49,397
Uh, ready, Mr. Executioner?

102
00:03:49,497 --> 00:03:52,500
-Yes, my liege.

103
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:56,070
-You have stolen painted
eggs in a time of famine.

104
00:03:56,170 --> 00:03:57,405
Uh, off with her head, Frank.

105
00:03:57,505 --> 00:04:00,140


106
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:02,043
And no chocolate mess.

107
00:04:02,143 --> 00:04:04,178
Well, poopsies?
[bots crying]

108
00:04:04,278 --> 00:04:07,182
-What about the pardon
from Fanny Farmer?

109
00:04:07,282 --> 00:04:08,248
-Yeah.

110
00:04:08,348 --> 00:04:10,952
His only crime was
being born delicious!

111
00:04:11,052 --> 00:04:12,120
-It's OK, you guys.

112
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:12,887
There, there.

113
00:04:12,987 --> 00:04:14,122
It's all right.

114
00:04:14,222 --> 00:04:16,090
Let's show them our invention
exchange, all right?

115
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:18,693
Our invention exchange
is called the Cartuner.

116
00:04:18,793 --> 00:04:20,795
Have you ever noticed
that funnies just

117
00:04:20,895 --> 00:04:22,363
aren't funny anymore?

118
00:04:22,463 --> 00:04:25,098
The Cartuner takes
stodgy, ambiguous cartoons

119
00:04:25,198 --> 00:04:28,202
like Mark Trail and mixes
them with stiflingly unfunny

120
00:04:28,302 --> 00:04:31,772
cartoons like Blondie, puts them
together and makes them funny.

121
00:04:31,838 --> 00:04:33,240
Check it out.

122
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:34,242
Well, look at this.

123
00:04:34,342 --> 00:04:34,942
Huh.

124
00:04:35,042 --> 00:04:35,510
Let's see what happened.

125
00:04:35,610 --> 00:04:36,910
Well, OK.

126
00:04:37,012 --> 00:04:40,113
And here's Dagwood, and he's
eating a giant sandwich made

127
00:04:40,213 --> 00:04:42,483
out of twigs, grubs,
and a tufted titmouse.

128
00:04:42,583 --> 00:04:43,350
[bots laughing]

129
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:44,285
-That's pretty funny.

130
00:04:44,385 --> 00:04:45,620
-Hey, that's funny.
-Oh, oh, oh!

131
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:47,388
Now do the Lockhorns and Cathy.
-Ooh, yeah.

132
00:04:47,488 --> 00:04:49,190
Yeah, yeah.
-OK, Lockhorns and Cathy.

133
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:50,725
Put them in the Cartuner.

134
00:04:50,825 --> 00:04:51,658
-It goes round and round.

135
00:04:51,758 --> 00:04:54,028
-Yes, and it comes out here.

136
00:04:54,128 --> 00:04:54,962
-Ah, let me see.

137
00:04:55,062 --> 00:04:56,497
Ah-- Oh ho, hey.

138
00:04:56,597 --> 00:04:59,200
Mrs. Lockhorn accidentally
crashed her car into the store

139
00:04:59,300 --> 00:05:01,302
where Cathy was
trying on a swimsuit!

140
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:05,138
[laughter]
-OK.

141
00:05:05,238 --> 00:05:06,473
-Hey, Joel.

142
00:05:06,573 --> 00:05:08,608
Uh, let's try a panel of Bil
Keane's heartwarming Family

143
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:11,145
Circus with Gary Larson's
off-center The Far Side.

144
00:05:11,245 --> 00:05:15,282
-OK, put them together, mix them
up, and we can-- and then, see,

145
00:05:15,383 --> 00:05:16,883
here it is.
Huh.

146
00:05:16,983 --> 00:05:18,218
-Oh, let me see there.

147
00:05:18,318 --> 00:05:21,222
A dotted trail showing where
anthropomorphic jackals have

148
00:05:21,322 --> 00:05:22,190
chased little Billy!

149
00:05:22,290 --> 00:05:23,123
-It's a hoot!

150
00:05:23,223 --> 00:05:24,558
[laughing]

151
00:05:24,658 --> 00:05:25,527
-Ah, the carnage!

152
00:05:25,627 --> 00:05:26,393
-What do you think, sirs?

153
00:05:26,493 --> 00:05:28,395
-Ahh-- my ribs.

154
00:05:28,495 --> 00:05:30,665
-I don't think, I get even.

155
00:05:30,765 --> 00:05:32,433
Dig this, mes amis.

156
00:05:32,533 --> 00:05:37,272
Your average Joe has never seen
a film like today's experiment.

157
00:05:37,372 --> 00:05:39,307
Your average person
on the street

158
00:05:39,407 --> 00:05:42,077
has not even begun to
conceptualize the horror which

159
00:05:42,177 --> 00:05:47,482
is your experiment
today, Mr. Joel Average.

160
00:05:47,582 --> 00:05:51,252
I give to you, "Manos--
The Hands of Fate."

161
00:05:51,352 --> 00:05:54,888
Served with a short, the
stirring conclusion to "Hired."

162
00:05:54,988 --> 00:05:55,657
Hit the button, Frank.

163
00:05:55,757 --> 00:05:56,357
-Yes.

164
00:05:56,457 --> 00:05:57,325
Oh, Fluffy, you're so--

165
00:05:57,425 --> 00:05:57,992
-Frank!

166
00:05:58,092 --> 00:06:00,193
--Oh, Fluffy, here.

167
00:06:00,293 --> 00:06:01,928
Here, you take him.

168
00:06:02,028 --> 00:06:07,068


169
00:06:07,168 --> 00:06:08,770
-Push the button, Frank!

170
00:06:08,870 --> 00:06:11,338
-Ziggy had Garfield neutered?

171
00:06:11,438 --> 00:06:13,573
Now that's funny!

172
00:06:13,673 --> 00:06:15,543
[buzzer alarm]

173
00:06:15,643 --> 00:06:29,390


174
00:06:29,490 --> 00:06:30,892
-Ah, look.

175
00:06:30,992 --> 00:06:33,693
"Hired 2-- Electric Boogaloo."

176
00:06:33,795 --> 00:06:35,630
-A Jam Handy to the rescue.

177
00:06:35,730 --> 00:06:38,332
-My Jam Handy.

178
00:06:38,432 --> 00:06:42,803
-No employees were killed
during the making of this film.

179
00:06:42,903 --> 00:06:46,040
-Young fellas today won't work
the way we did 11 years ago.

180
00:06:46,140 --> 00:06:47,375
-Previously, on "Hired."

181
00:06:47,475 --> 00:06:48,777
-Maybe not, son.

182
00:06:48,877 --> 00:06:52,347
And then again, maybe they're
working harder than you think.

183
00:06:52,447 --> 00:06:54,648
-I may be wonderful but
I think you're wrong.

184
00:06:54,748 --> 00:06:56,483
-You were a pretty
good man when you first

185
00:06:56,583 --> 00:06:57,885
started in this business.

186
00:06:57,985 --> 00:07:02,857
And you probably were, but
you owe a lot of your success

187
00:07:02,957 --> 00:07:05,493
to the way Harry
Carpenter worked with you

188
00:07:05,593 --> 00:07:08,062
-Give 'em hell, Harry.

189
00:07:08,162 --> 00:07:11,298
-He used to tell me about
the mistakes you made.

190
00:07:11,398 --> 00:07:13,700
You may not have known
it, but you really

191
00:07:13,802 --> 00:07:15,803
served am apprenticeship.

192
00:07:15,903 --> 00:07:19,107
-Work, booze, work--

193
00:07:19,207 --> 00:07:20,942
-In every business.

194
00:07:21,042 --> 00:07:22,910
-Yeah, I suppose so.

195
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:25,813
-Remember how closely he
worked with you the first year?

196
00:07:25,913 --> 00:07:26,613
Heh.

197
00:07:26,713 --> 00:07:27,715
He used to tell me about it.

198
00:07:27,815 --> 00:07:29,117
-He ripped you a
new one everyday.

199
00:07:29,217 --> 00:07:31,918
-Yeah, I remember how he used to
talk to me about my prospects.

200
00:07:32,018 --> 00:07:32,887
-Your prostate?

201
00:07:32,987 --> 00:07:35,122
-How I was to
handle some of them.

202
00:07:35,222 --> 00:07:36,457
But you know, I always
thought that that

203
00:07:36,557 --> 00:07:38,893
was because he knew you so well.

204
00:07:38,993 --> 00:07:41,695
Maybe he had more of a
personal interest in me.

205
00:07:41,795 --> 00:07:42,597
-Nonsense.

206
00:07:42,697 --> 00:07:43,965
-He hated you.

207
00:07:44,065 --> 00:07:44,998
-Not Harry.

208
00:07:45,098 --> 00:07:46,467
-Oh, he's having an episode.

209
00:07:46,567 --> 00:07:49,270
-He worked that hard with
every man he ever hired.

210
00:07:49,370 --> 00:07:50,470
-He got sent to jail for it.

211
00:07:50,572 --> 00:07:52,740
-He was smart enough to
realize that he knew more

212
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:56,843
about selling cars than
any man on his sales force.

213
00:07:56,943 --> 00:07:59,513
And that it took his
direction and leadership

214
00:07:59,613 --> 00:08:01,315
to get the most out of his men.

215
00:08:01,415 --> 00:08:02,015
-Ah!

216
00:08:02,115 --> 00:08:03,283
Flying elves are back.

217
00:08:03,383 --> 00:08:06,987
-And I don't believe men
have changed much since then.

218
00:08:07,087 --> 00:08:07,722
-Aah!

219
00:08:07,822 --> 00:08:10,090
-How about this Jimmy?

220
00:08:10,190 --> 00:08:11,258
Doesn't he work?

221
00:08:11,358 --> 00:08:13,693
Doesn't he get in for the
morning sales meetings?

222
00:08:13,793 --> 00:08:15,095
[hiccup]

223
00:08:15,195 --> 00:08:16,397
-Oh yeah, he's always punctual.

224
00:08:16,497 --> 00:08:18,598
He never misses.

225
00:08:18,698 --> 00:08:19,967
But take his
prospects, for example.

226
00:08:20,067 --> 00:08:21,035
-Please.
[imitates rimshot]

227
00:08:21,135 --> 00:08:22,303
-Why, I looked them
over the other day,

228
00:08:22,403 --> 00:08:25,305
and it's just about the
poorest list I've ever seen.

229
00:08:25,405 --> 00:08:26,573
I remember one of
the first things

230
00:08:26,673 --> 00:08:27,842
that Harry drilled in to me--

231
00:08:27,942 --> 00:08:29,110
-Was Harry!

232
00:08:29,210 --> 00:08:30,612
-The importance of having
a good list of prospects.

233
00:08:30,712 --> 00:08:31,745
-Yes?

234
00:08:31,845 --> 00:08:33,713
-Then he showed me how
to go about getting it.

235
00:08:33,813 --> 00:08:35,315
How to keep it up.

236
00:08:35,415 --> 00:08:36,283
How to cover the list.

237
00:08:36,383 --> 00:08:37,352
And how often.

238
00:08:37,452 --> 00:08:38,585
-Yeah, he performed
puppet shows for me.

239
00:08:38,685 --> 00:08:40,487
-I had the importance
of prospects drilled

240
00:08:40,588 --> 00:08:41,922
into me from the
very first day I

241
00:08:42,022 --> 00:08:42,990
-Whoa!
-Aah!

242
00:08:43,090 --> 00:08:43,657
-He's evil!

243
00:08:43,757 --> 00:08:44,592
-Mhm.

244
00:08:44,692 --> 00:08:46,360
-Has Jimmy?

245
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:48,662
-Has Jimmy what?

246
00:08:48,762 --> 00:08:52,433
-Had the importance of
prospects pounded into him

247
00:08:52,533 --> 00:08:55,002
since the first day
he started to work?

248
00:08:55,102 --> 00:08:56,837
-Well, I've told him
how important they are.

249
00:08:56,937 --> 00:08:59,207
-Yeah, but have
you shown him how,

250
00:08:59,307 --> 00:09:00,742
when and where to get them?

251
00:09:00,842 --> 00:09:02,643
Have you checked
on him every day

252
00:09:02,743 --> 00:09:04,045
to see that he did it right?

253
00:09:04,145 --> 00:09:04,878
-Have you hugged him?

254
00:09:04,978 --> 00:09:06,513
-Dad, I've got other work to do.

255
00:09:06,613 --> 00:09:09,450
-Harry wasn't too busy to
make a good man out of you.

256
00:09:09,550 --> 00:09:13,253
-Ah, you still got
a little-- eh-- hmm.

257
00:09:13,353 --> 00:09:15,857
-Say, I think I'm beginning
to see what you mean.

258
00:09:15,957 --> 00:09:18,258
-I'm beginning to sober
up and you're scaring me.

259
00:09:18,358 --> 00:09:19,327
-I wonder.

260
00:09:19,427 --> 00:09:21,628
-(SINGING) I wah,
wah, wah, wah, wonder.

261
00:09:21,728 --> 00:09:22,930
-Say, maybe that's
the reason I haven't

262
00:09:23,030 --> 00:09:24,365
had very much luck with Jimmy.

263
00:09:24,465 --> 00:09:25,700
-I'm gonna dance!

264
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:28,535
-Or with Henderson, that other
fellow I hired last year.

265
00:09:28,635 --> 00:09:29,970
You know, he looked
pretty good to me, too,

266
00:09:30,070 --> 00:09:31,205
Dad, when I first hired him.

267
00:09:31,305 --> 00:09:32,038
-Heh, who doesn't?

268
00:09:32,138 --> 00:09:34,642
-Certainly would be worth a try.

269
00:09:34,742 --> 00:09:35,342
-Yes, sir.

270
00:09:35,442 --> 00:09:36,710
-Don't hit me!

271
00:09:36,810 --> 00:09:38,112
-Come to think of it, the time
I'm spending on other things

272
00:09:38,212 --> 00:09:40,915
right now might be costing me
a whole lot more than I think.

273
00:09:41,015 --> 00:09:42,450
Men need leadership.

274
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:43,817
Leadership is important.

275
00:09:43,917 --> 00:09:45,920
Oh, I've always known
that, Dad, but I never

276
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:48,255
thought about in connection
with my own business.

277
00:09:48,355 --> 00:09:49,557
But starting Monday morning,

278
00:09:49,657 --> 00:09:50,423
-Don't touch me!

279
00:09:50,523 --> 00:09:51,858
-I'm gonna stir things up a bit.

280
00:09:51,958 --> 00:09:54,528
I'm going to show these fellas
what leadership really means.

281
00:09:54,628 --> 00:09:55,228
[hiccup]

282
00:09:55,328 --> 00:09:57,932
-And then we'll see.

283
00:09:58,032 --> 00:10:00,400
-We're going to have leadership
the way my old man told me.

284
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:01,935
You, put a handkerchief
on your head!

285
00:10:02,035 --> 00:10:03,770
You, swat at imaginary elves!

286
00:10:03,870 --> 00:10:07,442
You, rock on the
porch all night!

287
00:10:07,542 --> 00:10:09,577
-Sales are the most important
thing in this business.

288
00:10:09,677 --> 00:10:10,845
-Seeing as how we're
salesmen and all.

289
00:10:10,945 --> 00:10:12,313
-We're going to work
closer together than we

290
00:10:12,413 --> 00:10:14,015
have in the past in
order to get more sales.

291
00:10:14,115 --> 00:10:15,215
-But first, martinis!

292
00:10:15,315 --> 00:10:16,383
-Well, that's all
for this meeting,

293
00:10:16,483 --> 00:10:17,718
but I want to check
with each one of you

294
00:10:17,818 --> 00:10:20,153
personally before you
go out this morning.

295
00:10:20,253 --> 00:10:21,288
Jimmy, I'll talk with you first.

296
00:10:21,388 --> 00:10:22,557
-Because you've got
the most problems.

297
00:10:22,657 --> 00:10:25,525
-The meeting's adjourned.

298
00:10:25,625 --> 00:10:29,163
Jimmy, from what you say,
I'd put these two away

299
00:10:29,263 --> 00:10:30,197
for two weeks.
-Whack 'em.

300
00:10:30,297 --> 00:10:31,598
Whack 'em good.

301
00:10:31,698 --> 00:10:33,733
-Your time is too valuable to
be spending on them right now.

302
00:10:33,833 --> 00:10:35,035
-Yeah, mhm.

303
00:10:35,135 --> 00:10:38,405
-And then I'd take these
four in this order.

304
00:10:38,505 --> 00:10:40,440
-Uh, boss, will you help
me with my soapbox racer?

305
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:42,977
-It'll save you time
in covering them.

306
00:10:43,077 --> 00:10:44,645
Now have you got any others
up in this end of town

307
00:10:44,745 --> 00:10:45,545
when you get up there?

308
00:10:45,645 --> 00:10:46,247
-Uh, no sir.

309
00:10:46,347 --> 00:10:47,548
I don't believe I have.

310
00:10:47,648 --> 00:10:48,515
-All right.

311
00:10:48,615 --> 00:10:49,950
-Here's a lollipop, Jimmy.

312
00:10:50,050 --> 00:10:52,152
-I'll give you the names
of three Chevrolet owners

313
00:10:52,252 --> 00:10:53,220
who live up that way.

314
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:55,022
-Crush 'em.

315
00:10:55,122 --> 00:10:59,427
-And all three of them
should be about ready to buy.

316
00:10:59,527 --> 00:11:01,562
-Meanwhile, Eliot Ness
and his Untouchables

317
00:11:01,662 --> 00:11:02,830
head for a speakeasy in Berwin.

318
00:11:02,930 --> 00:11:06,733


319
00:11:06,833 --> 00:11:09,037
[gasps]

320
00:11:09,137 --> 00:11:12,072
-Sintar gets the most sales
because he's a shape shifter.

321
00:11:12,172 --> 00:11:14,242
-Harry, I don't think you
drove quite far enough

322
00:11:14,342 --> 00:11:16,377
with that prospect before
you let him take the wheel.

323
00:11:16,477 --> 00:11:18,712
-Yes Mr. Warren, but
he bought the car.

324
00:11:18,812 --> 00:11:20,413
-I know, I know,
but I'm speaking

325
00:11:20,513 --> 00:11:22,182
about your demonstrations
in general.

326
00:11:22,282 --> 00:11:23,450
-But he bought the flippin' car!

327
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:25,452
-You like to turn the
wheel over to your prospect

328
00:11:25,552 --> 00:11:27,020
pretty early in the
demonstration ride.

329
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:28,288
-I know, but--

330
00:11:28,388 --> 00:11:29,623
-Now, you know, of course
that when you drive first,

331
00:11:29,723 --> 00:11:31,058
-Yeah, but--

332
00:11:31,158 --> 00:11:33,227
-The prospect almost invariably
drives back over the same route

333
00:11:33,327 --> 00:11:34,062
that you took.

334
00:11:34,162 --> 00:11:35,395
-Yeah, but he bought the car!

335
00:11:35,495 --> 00:11:37,030
-And if you'd driven on
to that rough stretch

336
00:11:37,130 --> 00:11:38,932
up on Summit
Avenue, you wouldn't

337
00:11:39,032 --> 00:11:40,133
have to direct
him over it later.

338
00:11:40,233 --> 00:11:41,468
-He bought the car.

339
00:11:41,568 --> 00:11:43,470
-That'll give you a chance to
have him keep his mind more

340
00:11:43,570 --> 00:11:45,438
on the car than on the road.

341
00:11:45,538 --> 00:11:47,508
-Uh, you got a
nice office, boss.

342
00:11:47,608 --> 00:11:49,643
-It's usually best to--
I'll go along with you again

343
00:11:49,743 --> 00:11:51,712
this morning, Jimmy, on
these first two calls.

344
00:11:51,812 --> 00:11:53,580
-Gee, that'll be
swell, Mr. Warren.

345
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:55,315
I'll sure appreciate your help.

346
00:11:55,415 --> 00:11:57,518
I always learn something
too, when we go out together.

347
00:11:57,618 --> 00:11:59,453
-Yeah, maybe I can kiss your
butt on the way out, hmm?

348
00:11:59,553 --> 00:12:00,420
How about that?
Heh?

349
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:02,255
He he.

350
00:12:02,355 --> 00:12:04,192
-Uh, just gotta push my
family out of the way.

351
00:12:04,292 --> 00:12:05,625
I kinda live out
of my car, if you

352
00:12:05,725 --> 00:12:08,962
know what I mean, there, just--

353
00:12:09,062 --> 00:12:11,698
-So, that's when I got into
commemorative spoon collecting.

354
00:12:11,798 --> 00:12:12,533
You?

355
00:12:12,633 --> 00:12:16,437
-Why don't you punch it, Jimmy.

356
00:12:16,537 --> 00:12:19,573
I wanna be in Tijuana by
8 o'clock or you're fired.

357
00:12:19,673 --> 00:12:21,008
-Oh, they're meeting
with Floyd the Barber.

358
00:12:21,108 --> 00:12:23,543
-(IMITATING FLOYD) Oh, oh, the
car is so wonderful, oh, oh!

359
00:12:23,643 --> 00:12:26,213


360
00:12:26,313 --> 00:12:28,215
-Now listen, I said
no you two yesterday.

361
00:12:28,315 --> 00:12:29,617
-But ma'am, do you
know that Chevrolet

362
00:12:29,717 --> 00:12:31,785
has a wonderful
plan for your life?

363
00:12:31,885 --> 00:12:35,688
-Why'd you bring the greasy guy?

364
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:37,858
-Better see them again tomorrow.

365
00:12:37,958 --> 00:12:40,060
And be sure to have Mrs. Beckham
into the showroom tonight

366
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:40,995
with her husband.

367
00:12:41,095 --> 00:12:42,295
-OK, I'll sure do it.

368
00:12:42,395 --> 00:12:44,165
Thanks again for your
help, Mr. Warren.

369
00:12:44,265 --> 00:12:45,398
-Uh, may I call you?

370
00:12:45,498 --> 00:12:48,168
I'd like to see
ya again sometime.

371
00:12:48,268 --> 00:12:52,640
-Are you now, or have you
ever been, a Ford owner?

372
00:12:52,740 --> 00:12:54,775
-I wouldn't buy a car from
you if you were the last man

373
00:12:54,875 --> 00:12:57,143
on Earth, you sorry sack of--

374
00:12:57,243 --> 00:12:59,380
-That's where your truck
data book comes in.

375
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:00,780
-Drug data?

376
00:13:00,882 --> 00:13:02,817
-You'll find the information you
want on that special equipment

377
00:13:02,917 --> 00:13:03,683
right back here.

378
00:13:03,783 --> 00:13:04,985
-Uh, somewheres.

379
00:13:05,085 --> 00:13:07,822
-Now, show this information
to your prospect, too.

380
00:13:07,922 --> 00:13:10,423
Just don't keep glancing at it
and then telling them about it.

381
00:13:10,523 --> 00:13:12,125
Let them read it.

382
00:13:12,225 --> 00:13:17,230
Very often, the printed word is
a big help in closing the sale.

383
00:13:17,330 --> 00:13:20,633
-Oh, look, it's
salesman-parent conferences.

384
00:13:20,733 --> 00:13:23,437
-Well, my life is a hollow lie.

385
00:13:23,537 --> 00:13:25,772
-Just too many of them
on hand now, George.

386
00:13:25,873 --> 00:13:27,007
That's the reason
we couldn't afford

387
00:13:27,107 --> 00:13:28,542
to offer a higher appraisal.

388
00:13:28,642 --> 00:13:31,145
-Yeah, I can see your
side of the picture.

389
00:13:31,245 --> 00:13:33,247
-Yes, but there's a
good side too, George.

390
00:13:33,347 --> 00:13:36,383
For example, right now we
haven't a single '39 Plymouth

391
00:13:36,483 --> 00:13:37,818
four-door on the lot.

392
00:13:37,918 --> 00:13:39,587
And that means we can
afford to make somebody

393
00:13:39,687 --> 00:13:40,888
a pretty good offer.

394
00:13:40,988 --> 00:13:43,423
-Say, I've got a prospect
downtown with one of those.

395
00:13:43,523 --> 00:13:45,058
-Not too hot, but I was
going to get too him

396
00:13:45,158 --> 00:13:46,260
again in the next few days.

397
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:47,662
-Fine, get him in here today.

398
00:13:47,762 --> 00:13:49,263
And I'm sure we can make
them an offer that'll

399
00:13:49,363 --> 00:13:50,597
close a deal for you.

400
00:13:50,697 --> 00:13:51,498
Besides, we can use--

401
00:13:51,598 --> 00:13:52,800
[gasps]

402
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:54,435
-Mr. Milton, you can depend
on what our Mr. Hill here

403
00:13:54,535 --> 00:13:56,137
has just told you.

404
00:13:56,237 --> 00:13:57,672
And I'll give you my
own personal word,

405
00:13:57,772 --> 00:13:59,773
too, that if you have any
trouble along that line,

406
00:13:59,873 --> 00:14:00,875
we'll make it good.

407
00:14:00,975 --> 00:14:01,542
[snickering]

408
00:14:01,642 --> 00:14:03,177
-Well, I don't know.

409
00:14:03,277 --> 00:14:06,047
The last two cars I bought,
when they had trouble,

410
00:14:06,147 --> 00:14:08,148
it was always my
fault, never the car's.

411
00:14:08,248 --> 00:14:09,750
-We want you to be
just as satisfied

412
00:14:09,850 --> 00:14:11,752
with your new Chevrolet
next year, Mr. Milton,

413
00:14:11,852 --> 00:14:12,920
as you will be next week.

414
00:14:13,020 --> 00:14:13,953
-That's exactly right.

415
00:14:14,053 --> 00:14:15,322
-Just buy the car!

416
00:14:15,422 --> 00:14:17,090
-We couldn't have afforded to
make the investment we have

417
00:14:17,190 --> 00:14:19,927
here unless we gave the
kind of satisfaction that

418
00:14:20,027 --> 00:14:23,363
make our customers come
back to us year after year.

419
00:14:23,463 --> 00:14:26,200
We want you to be a regular
customer, too, Mr. Milton.

420
00:14:26,300 --> 00:14:28,802
In fact, we hope you'll buy
your next ten cars from us.

421
00:14:28,902 --> 00:14:29,937
-Ten cars?

422
00:14:30,037 --> 00:14:31,272
-That's why we'll give
you the kind of service

423
00:14:31,372 --> 00:14:33,840
it takes on this one to bring
you back for the next one.

424
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:36,010
And the next, and
the next, so on.

425
00:14:36,110 --> 00:14:37,778
You see, that's the only
way we can hope to--

426
00:14:37,878 --> 00:14:41,582
-How can I keep
this from Gladys?

427
00:14:41,682 --> 00:14:44,285
-Adlai Stevenson buys a car.

428
00:14:44,385 --> 00:14:46,287
-Good salesman.

429
00:14:46,387 --> 00:14:49,457
-Bad salesman.

430
00:14:49,557 --> 00:14:52,425
-(IMITATES JIMMY DURANTE)
Inka dinka do, ha ha ha ha ha!

431
00:14:52,525 --> 00:14:54,027
-Now, if you want to
see your daughter again,

432
00:14:54,127 --> 00:14:55,262
you'll do what I--

433
00:14:55,362 --> 00:14:56,430
-And now let us pray.

434
00:14:56,530 --> 00:14:57,865
Lord!

435
00:14:57,965 --> 00:14:59,767
-Huh?

436
00:14:59,867 --> 00:15:00,800
-You could hurt me?

437
00:15:00,900 --> 00:15:06,307
-(ANGRILY) Would you sign it?

438
00:15:06,407 --> 00:15:07,007
Just sign it!

439
00:15:07,107 --> 00:15:08,642
Sign it!

440
00:15:08,742 --> 00:15:13,180
-Well, can I keep the pen?

441
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:14,482
-I was kidding.

442
00:15:14,582 --> 00:15:17,517
I was going to sign when I
walked in here, ha, you nuts.

443
00:15:17,618 --> 00:15:21,988
-Christy ahead at the break,
Dunn and Stewart close behind.

444
00:15:22,088 --> 00:15:22,823
-Huh?

445
00:15:22,923 --> 00:15:24,458
-Believe me, leadership pays.

446
00:15:24,558 --> 00:15:25,592
-Is he talking to us?

447
00:15:25,692 --> 00:15:26,627
-Mhm.
-Yeah.

448
00:15:26,727 --> 00:15:27,593
-First, hire good men.

449
00:15:27,695 --> 00:15:28,928
-Make sure they're clean.

450
00:15:29,028 --> 00:15:30,930
-Then see that they're well
trained on their own product

451
00:15:31,032 --> 00:15:33,567
and on competitive products.

452
00:15:33,667 --> 00:15:34,502
Second,

453
00:15:34,602 --> 00:15:35,502
-Check their teeth.

454
00:15:35,602 --> 00:15:36,803
-Be sure they have
all the equipment

455
00:15:36,903 --> 00:15:40,540
and selling helps they need
and know how to use them.

456
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:41,508
-Fifth--
-Third,

457
00:15:41,608 --> 00:15:42,442
-Oh, right, third.

458
00:15:42,542 --> 00:15:43,643
-Help them plan
their work to make

459
00:15:43,743 --> 00:15:46,847
the most effective
use of their time.

460
00:15:46,947 --> 00:15:48,815
Fourth,
-Live at home until you're 40.

461
00:15:48,915 --> 00:15:50,950
-Stay in close touch
with every man.

462
00:15:51,052 --> 00:15:52,787
Know what each one is doing.

463
00:15:52,887 --> 00:15:53,653
Work with them.

464
00:15:53,753 --> 00:15:54,688
-Name names.

465
00:15:54,788 --> 00:15:57,257
-And fifth, keep up
their enthusiasm.

466
00:15:57,357 --> 00:15:58,092
-En-too-zee-az-um.

467
00:15:58,192 --> 00:16:00,760
-Encourage every man every day.

468
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:02,062
-Give 'em backrubs.

469
00:16:02,162 --> 00:16:03,097
-And never forget,

470
00:16:03,197 --> 00:16:04,197
-I love you.

471
00:16:04,297 --> 00:16:08,535
-As I did, that people
who represent you--

472
00:16:08,635 --> 00:16:09,403
-It's Dr. Giggles!

473
00:16:09,503 --> 00:16:10,703
-Need your help.
-Goodnight.

474
00:16:10,803 --> 00:16:13,040
Stay pink, soft and oily.

475
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:15,575
-(SINGING) We're hired!
-Hired!

476
00:16:15,675 --> 00:16:30,857


477
00:16:30,957 --> 00:16:34,562
-Yes, it's Manos,
the Hands of Fate.

478
00:16:34,662 --> 00:16:39,767
-You know, this is the slowest
car chase scene I've ever seen.

479
00:16:39,867 --> 00:16:41,668
-Like father, like son.

480
00:16:41,768 --> 00:16:43,003
Think about it, won't you?

481
00:16:43,103 --> 00:16:48,908


482
00:16:49,008 --> 00:16:50,310
-This is where you
get out, honey.

483
00:16:50,410 --> 00:16:51,578
-We're almost there, honey.

484
00:16:51,678 --> 00:16:53,780
Just a little while longer
and your vacation starts.

485
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:56,317
-I've been cold, Mother.

486
00:16:56,417 --> 00:16:59,720
-Visit beautiful ground zero.

487
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:01,288
-Hey, look.

488
00:17:01,388 --> 00:17:02,857
Poodle bites, poodle chews.

489
00:17:02,957 --> 00:17:07,728
-(IN CHILD'S VOICE) His
heartbeat's irregular.

490
00:17:07,828 --> 00:17:09,228
-We should be pretty
close right now.

491
00:17:09,328 --> 00:17:11,332
The agent said it was about
12 miles from Highway 10

492
00:17:11,432 --> 00:17:13,600
and that was Highway
10 back there.

493
00:17:13,700 --> 00:17:14,802
-But you know, we
should've asked

494
00:17:14,902 --> 00:17:17,170
for better directions
at the last gas station.

495
00:17:17,270 --> 00:17:19,172
-Listen, I've never
gotten us lost before.

496
00:17:19,272 --> 00:17:19,873
-Have I?

497
00:17:19,973 --> 00:17:22,708
Honey?

498
00:17:22,810 --> 00:17:25,112
-Mommy, I'm cold.

499
00:17:25,212 --> 00:17:26,813
-(IN CHILD'S VOICE)My core
temperature's dropped.

500
00:17:26,913 --> 00:17:27,680
-OK, Debbie.

501
00:17:27,782 --> 00:17:29,015
I'll put the top up.

502
00:17:29,115 --> 00:17:29,950
-Nevermind, Mike.

503
00:17:30,050 --> 00:17:31,718
Debbie can come
up sit between us.

504
00:17:31,818 --> 00:17:33,853
-You can have the top up
or a birthday present.

505
00:17:33,953 --> 00:17:35,055
It's your choice, honey.

506
00:17:35,155 --> 00:17:37,758


507
00:17:37,858 --> 00:17:40,060
-Ah, what do you
want, a hug next?

508
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:41,128
Jeez, kids.

509
00:17:41,228 --> 00:17:43,297
-Cousin Itt!
[imitates cousin itt sounds]

510
00:17:43,397 --> 00:17:46,167
-Why don't we sing a song
to help pass the time?

511
00:17:46,267 --> 00:17:49,903
-(IN CHILD'S VOICE)Let's sing
